<h2>The order in brief</h2>
<p>The petitioner applied for bail under Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 before the Calcutta High Court, where the matter was heard by Justice Tirthankar Ghosh. The case, registered at Kushmandi Police Station in July 2026, invokes a battery of NDPS Act provisions — Sections 21(c), 22(c), 23(c), 25, 27A, 28 and 29 — centring on an alleged recovery of 199 bottles of codeine-based cough mixture. Her counsel pressed two points: the recovery was effected at the house of another accused, and she had already been in custody for four months. The State opposed bail and placed a memo of evidence, which the Court directed be kept on record. The outcome: bail rejected, with the observation that the petitioner should renew her prayer after the chargesheet is submitted.</p>

<h2>Why courts defer NDPS bail to the chargesheet stage</h2>
<p>Orders of this kind are common in NDPS practice, and they rest on an intelligible logic rather than mere caution. Three strands usually combine.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The investigation is the evidence.</strong> At the pre-chargesheet stage the court has only the FIR, the seizure papers and the diary. Whether the prosecution can actually connect the accused to contraband recovered elsewhere is unknowable until the investigation&rsquo;s product is filed.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 37 looms over commercial quantities.</strong> Where offences under Sections 19, 24 or 27A, or involving commercial quantity, are alleged, the twin conditions of Section 37 NDPS Act require the court to record a prima facie satisfaction of innocence — hard to record responsibly on an incomplete record.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Early release can distort the probe.</strong> Courts worry that releasing an accused mid-investigation in syndicate-type allegations (Section 29 conspiracy was invoked here) risks interference with searches, financial tracing and the identification of other participants.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the defence gains from a timing rejection</h2>
<p>A rejection &ldquo;at this stage&rdquo; is a materially different thing from a rejection on merits. The Calcutta order records no adverse finding: nothing on conscious possession, nothing on quantity, nothing on the Section 37 bar. When the chargesheet arrives, the renewed application starts clean, and the defence gains concrete material to work with — the forensic report on the seized mixture and its codeine concentration, the statements connecting (or failing to connect) the petitioner to the co-accused&rsquo;s premises, and the completeness of compliance with the NDPS Act&rsquo;s seizure and sampling safeguards.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Rejection on merits</strong><p>Court records findings on possession, quantity or Section 37 — a later bench must be shown changed circumstances to depart from them.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Rejection on timing</strong><p>Court records no merits finding; the chargesheet itself is the changed circumstance, and the renewed plea is heard on a full record.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Ask the court to record, as here, that the rejection is &ldquo;at this stage&rdquo; and that liberty is granted to renew after the chargesheet — language that neutralises the order in future hearings.</p>
  <p>Track the statutory default-bail clock under Section 187 BNSS separately: for several NDPS offences, Section 36A(4) of the NDPS Act extends the investigation period, and the extension mechanics deserve independent scrutiny.</p>
  <p>Where recovery is from a co-accused&rsquo;s premises, build the renewed application around the chargesheet&rsquo;s actual connecting material, not around denials.</p>
  <p>Preserve custody-period arithmetic: prolonged incarceration with a distant trial is itself a recognised ground that strengthens with time.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information only. It is not legal advice, and it makes no comment on the merits of the pending investigation.</div>
